            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years and Education</strong><br>\nIvan Pavlov was born on September 14, 1849, in Ryazan, Russia. Initially enrolled in a theological seminary, Pavlov abandoned a religious career to pursue science, and he never completed his religious training. He graduated from the University of St. Petersburg with a degree in natural sciences.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Research Beginnings</strong><br>\nPavlov's early research focused on the physiology of the circulatory and digestive systems. His work on the latter, particularly the study of gastric function, won him the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1904.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of Classical Conditioning</strong><br>\nWhile researching salivation in dogs as part of his digestive research, Pavlov observed that the dogs would begin to salivate before food was actually presented to them. This led to his groundbreaking work in classical conditioning.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Experimentation Techniques</strong><br>\nPavlov's experimentation with dogs led to the concept of the \"conditioned reflex.\" He used a variety of stimuli, including bells, metronomes, and electric shocks, to study the acquisition, strength, and extinction of conditioned responses.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Psychology and Education</strong><br>\nAlthough a physiologist by trade, Pavlov's work had profound implications for psychology. His findings on conditioning have influenced educational practices and treatments for phobias and addictions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pavlov's Scientific Approach</strong><br>\nPavlov was known for his insistence on objective study of observable phenomena, resisting the inclusion of subjective interpretations. His empirical approach set a standard for experimental procedures in various scientific disciplines.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
