          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Publications and Legacy</strong><br>\nPavlov's works, such as \"Lectures on Conditioned Reflexes,\" have had a lasting impact on both physiology and psychology. His research methods and findings on conditioned reflexes are still taught and referenced in contemporary science and education.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal and Political Life</strong><br>\nThroughout his life, Pavlov was critical of communism, which came to rule Russia after the 1917 Revolution. Despite his criticisms, the Soviet government funded his research, valuing its prestige.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Memoriam</strong><br>\nPavlov died in Leningrad (now St. Petersburg) on February 27, 1936. His work continues to be honored, and the term \"Pavlovian\" is synonymous with the concept of conditioned response in the study of behavior.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
